/** @page qore_regex Regular Expressions

    @tableofcontents

    @section qore_regex_intro Qore Regular Expression Introduction

    Regular expression functionality in %Qore is provided by
    <a href="http://www.pcre.org">PCRE: Perl-Compatible Regular Expression library</a>.

    Using this library, %Qore implements regular expression pattern matching using very simple syntax with semantics
    similar to those of <a href="http://www.perl.org">Perl 5</a>.
    One difference between %Qore and Perl to keep in mind is that @ref qore_regex_backreferences "backreferences" in
    %Qore are referenced as \c $1, \c $2, \c $3, etc which differs from Perl's syntax (which uses numbered backslashes
    instead).

    @par Examples:
    @code{.py}
    # call process() if the string starts with an alphanumeric character
    if (str =~ /^[[:alnum:]]/)
        process(str);
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # example of using regular expressions in a switch statement
    switch (str) {
        case /^[^[:alnum:]]/: return True;
        case /^[0-9]/: return False;
        default: throw "ERROR", sprintf("invalid string %y", str);
    }
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # regular expression substitution + ignore case & global options
    str =~ s/abc/xyz/gi;
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # prefix all non-alphanumeric characters with a backslash
    str =~ s/([^[:alnum:]])/\\$1/g;
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # regular expression substring extraction
    *list l = (str =~ x/(?:(\w+):(\w+):)(\w+)/);
    @endcode

    @section qore_regex_operators Qore Regular Expression Operators

    The following is a list of operators based on regular expressions (or similar to regular expressions in the case
    of the transliteration operator).

    <b>Regular Expression Operators</b>
    |!Operator|!Description
    |@ref regex_match_operator|Returns @ref True "True" if the regular expression matches a string
    |@ref regex_no_match_operator|Returns @ref True "True" if the regular expression does not match a string
    |@ref regex_subst_operator|Substitutes text in a string based on matching a regular expression
    |@ref regex_extract_operator|Returns a list of substrings in a string based on matching patterns defined by a \
        regular expression
    |@ref transliteration_operator|Not a regular expression operator; transliterates one or more characters to other \
        characters in a string

    See the table below for valid regular expression options.

    @subsection qore_regex_options Qore Regular Expression Operator Options

    <b>Regular Expression Options</b>
    |!Option|!Description
    |\c i|Ignores case when matching
    |\c m|makes start-of-line (<tt>^</tt>) or end-of-line (<tt>$</tt>) match after or before any newline in the \
        subject string
    |\c s|makes a dot (<tt>.</tt>) match a newline character
    |\c x|ignores whitespace characters and enables comments prefixed by <tt>#</tt>
    |\c u|extends Posix character matching to Unicode characters
    |\c g|makes global substitutions or global extractions (only applicable with the substitution and extraction \
        operators)
    |\c U|makes quantifiers ungreedy by default; individual quantifiers can be made greedy by appending \c ? \
        (since %Qore 2.3)

    @section qore_regex_brace_delimiters Brace-Delimited Regular Expressions

    In addition to the traditional slash (<tt>/</tt>) delimiter, %Qore supports brace (<tt>{}</tt>) delimiters
    for regular expressions.  This is particularly useful when patterns contain literal forward slashes (such as
    URLs or file paths), avoiding the need to escape them.

    @note The \c m prefix is required for brace-delimited match expressions; bare <tt>{</tt> is not supported
    to avoid ambiguity with block statements.

    <b>Brace-Delimited Syntax</b>
    |!Operation|!Syntax|!Example
    |Match|\c m{pattern}flags|<tt>str =~ m{https://example\\.com}</tt>
    |Substitution|\c s{pattern}{replacement}flags|<tt>str =~ s{http://old\\.com}{https://new.com}g</tt>
    |Extraction|\c x{pattern}flags|<tt>l = (str =~ x{(\\w+)://(\\w+)})</tt>
    |Transliteration|\c tr{source}{target}|<tt>str =~ tr{a-z}{A-Z}</tt>
    |Case match|\c case \c m{pattern}:|<tt>case m{\\d{3}}:</tt>

    Brace nesting in patterns is handled automatically — PCRE2 quantifiers like <tt>\\d{3}</tt> or
    <tt>a{2,5}</tt> work correctly because the scanner tracks brace depth.  Escaped braces (<tt>\\{</tt> and
    <tt>\\}</tt>) do not affect depth tracking.

    @par Examples:
    @code{.py}
    # URL matching — no need to escape slashes
    if (url =~ m{https?://[\w.]+/\w+})
        process(url);
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # URL substitution
    str =~ s{http://old\.example\.com}{https://new.example.com}g;
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # quantifiers work naturally with brace delimiters
    if (str =~ m{^\d{3}-\d{4}$})
        printf("phone number: %s\n", str);
    @endcode

    @note Inside <tt>[...]</tt> character classes, use <tt>\\{</tt> and <tt>\\}</tt> instead of literal braces
    when using brace delimiters, since the scanner does not track character class boundaries.

    @since %Qore 2.3

    @section qore_regex_functions Qore Regular Expression Functions

    The following is a list of functions providing regular expression functionality where the pattern may be given at
    run-time:

    <b>Regular Expression Functions</b>
    |!Function|!Description
    |regex()|Returns @ref True "True" if the regular expression matches a string
    |regex_subst()|Substitutes a pattern in a string based on regular expressions and returns the new string; \
        also supports a callback variant for dynamic replacements (since %Qore 2.3)
    |regex_extract()|Returns a list of substrings in a string based on matching patterns defined by a regular \
        expression
    |regex_extract_named()|Returns named capture groups as a hash (since %Qore 2.3)
    |regex_extract_detailed()|Returns detailed match info including positions, groups, and named groups as a \
        list of @ref Qore::RegexMatchInfo "RegexMatchInfo" hashes (since %Qore 2.3)
    |regex_count()|Returns the number of non-overlapping matches (since %Qore 2.3)
    |regex_escape()|Escapes all non-alphanumeric characters for use as a literal regex pattern (since %Qore 2.3)

    @section qore_regex_escape_patterns Qore Regular Expression Escape Codes

    Escape characters in the pattern string are processed by the <a href="http://www.pcre.org">PCRE</a> library
    similar to how Perl5 handles escape characters.

    @subsection qore_regex_escape_replacement_string Qore Regular Expression Replacement String Escape Codes

    Regular expression substitution expressions have the following pattern:
    - <tt>s/</tt><i>&lt;pattern&gt;</i><tt>/</tt><i>&lt;replacement&gt;</i><tt>/</tt>

    The escape codes in the following table are supported in the replacement string.

    <b>Regular Expression Replacement String Escape Codes</b>
    |!Escape|!ASCII|!Decimal|!Octal|!Hex|!Description
    |\\a|\c BEL|\c 7|\c 007|\c 07|alarm or bell
    |\\b|\c BS|\c 8|\c 010|\c 08|backspace
    |\\e|\c ESC|\c 27|\c 033|\c 1B|escape character
    |\\f|\c FF|\c 12|\c 014|\c 0C|form feed
    |\\n|\c LF|\c 10|\c 012|\c 0A|line feed
    |\\r|\c CR|\c 13|\c 015|\c 0D|carriage return
    |\\t|\c HT|\c 9|\c 011|\c 09|horizontal tab
    |\\v|\c VT|\c 11|\c 013|\c 0B|vertical tab
    |\\$|\c $|\c 36|\c 044|\c 24|a literal dollar sign character
    |\\\\|\c \\|\c 134|\c 092|\c 5C|a literal backslash character
    |\\[0-7][0-7][0-7]|-|-|-|-|the ASCII character represented by the octal code

    Otherwise any backslashes in the replacement string will be copied literally to the output string.

    @section qore_regex_backreferences Qore Regular Expression Backreferences

    Qore uses <tt>$</tt><i>num</i> for backreferences in regular expression substitution expressions.  The first
    backreference is \c $1, the second $2, and so on.  \c $0 refers to the entire match (since %Qore 2.3).

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
    # prefix all non-alphanumeric characters with a backslash
    str =~ s/([^[:alnum:]])/\\$1/g;
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # remove parentheses from string at the beginning of the line
    str =~ s/^\((.*)\)/$1/;
    @endcode
    @code{.py}
    # wrap matched text using $0 (full match backreference)
    str =~ s/\w+/[$0]/g;  # "hello world" -> "[hello] [world]"
    @endcode

    @section qore_regex_named_groups Named Capture Groups

    %Qore supports PCRE2 named capture groups using \c (?<name>...) or \c (?P<name>...) syntax.  Named groups
    can be extracted as hash values using regex_extract_named(), the @ref Qore::Regex::extractNamedGroups()
    "Regex::extractNamedGroups()" method, or the @ref Qore::RegexExtract::extractNamedGroups()
    "RegexExtract::extractNamedGroups()" method.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
    auto h = regex_extract_named("2026-04-08", "(?<year>\\d{4})-(?<month>\\d{2})-(?<day>\\d{2})");
    # h = {"year": "2026", "month": "04", "day": "08"}
    @endcode

    Use the @ref RE_DupNames constant to allow duplicate named subpatterns in the same regex.

    @since %Qore 2.3

    @section qore_regex_detailed_extraction Detailed Match Extraction

    The @ref Qore::RegexMatchInfo "RegexMatchInfo" hashdecl provides detailed information about each match including
    the full match text, byte offset, length, captured groups, and named groups.  Use regex_extract_detailed(),
    @ref Qore::Regex::extractDetailed() "Regex::extractDetailed()", or
    <tt>\<string\>::regexExtractDetailed()</tt> to get this information.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
    *list<hash<RegexMatchInfo>> matches = regex_extract_detailed("abc 123 def 456", "\\d+", RE_Global);
    # matches[0] = {"match": "123", "offset": 4, "length": 3}
    # matches[1] = {"match": "456", "offset": 12, "length": 3}
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 2.3

    @section qore_regex_callback_subst Callback-Based Substitution

    The regex_subst() function and the @ref Qore::RegexSubst::subst() "RegexSubst::subst()" method support a
    callback variant where a closure or call reference computes the replacement string for each match.  The
    callback receives a @ref Qore::RegexMatchInfo "RegexMatchInfo" hash.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
    string result = regex_subst("abc 123 def 456", "\\d+", string sub (hash<RegexMatchInfo> m) {
        return string(int(m.match) * 2);
    }, RE_Global);
    # result = "abc 246 def 912"
    @endcode

    @since %Qore 2.3
*/
